\chapter{Formal Verification Framework}
\label{ch:verification}

To guarantee microkernel-grade security alongside bare-metal performance,
Telix employs a layered formal verification strategy. The verification
architecture establishes a formal linkage between
\textbf{hardware state dynamics} (ISA state transitions,
page table walk invariants, relaxed memory semantics) and
\textbf{kernel memory safety} (type preservation, encapsulation, and
data-race freedom).

Unlike prior formally verified kernels (seL4, CertiKOS) which required
approximately 25 person-years to verify a single implementation against a single
ISA, Telix's verification architecture exploits three structural advantages:
(1) the hardware model and its coherence theorems are already proven
independently of any kernel implementation (the \textsc{Tessera} project,
\S\ref{sec:hw_proofs}); (2) the framekernel's design choices reduce the
kernel's state space to a bounded set of page-table and TLB-shootdown
protocols; (3) a unifying proof framework—Iris separation logic—spans both
the hardware model and the kernel code, enabling direct composition.

\section{The Unified Verification Stack}
\label{sec:unified_stack}

Telix's verification architecture composes four independently developed,
formally connected layers:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{ISA Semantics (Sail \& upstream proofs).}
    RISC-V and ARMv8 instruction semantics are specified in the Sail
    language \footcite{armstrong19} and mechanically translated to Rocq
    (formerly Coq). The upstream sail-riscv and sail-arm models carry
    independent verification by the REMS project. Telix's hardware
    model connects to these via conformance bridge lemmas
    (\S\ref{sec:hw_proofs}).

  \item \textbf{Hardware Model (Tessera).}
    A multi-architecture machine model—the MMU, TLBs, page tables,
    IOMMU, interrupt controller, timer, and device topology—is specified
    in Sail, mechanically generated to Rocq, and proven correct with
    \textasciitilde{}12,000 lines of machine-checked proofs
    (\S\ref{sec:hw_proofs}).

  \item \textbf{Machine Interface Iris Layer.}
    The hardware model's invariants—PTE ownership, TLB coherence,
    IOMMU mapping integrity—are lifted into Iris separation-logic
    resources (\S\ref{sec:machine_interface}). These resources
    constitute the bridge between the hardware proofs and any kernel
    implementation.

  \item \textbf{Kernel Verification (manual Iris heap\_lang).}
    The framekernel's page-table manipulation, TLB shootdown, IPI
    protocol, IOMMU management, and scheduler loop are specified and
    proved manually in Iris heap\_lang.  Rather than relying on an
    automatic Rust-to-Coq translation (RefinedRust) whose bare-metal
    compatibility is unproven, the kernel spec is written directly in
    the Iris language and proved against the machine interface
    resources (\S\ref{sec:kernel_verification}).
\end{enumerate}

