\section{Hardware-Integrity Proofs (Tessera)}
\label{sec:hw_proofs}

The \textsc{Tessera} project provides the hardware-side formal guarantees
on which Telix's kernel proofs rest. All theorems are machine-checked in
Rocq and closed under the global context (axiom-free).\footnote{\fullcite{tessera25}}

\subsection{Page-Table Coherence (G1--G2)}

The central invariant linking kernel operations to hardware state:

\[
\forall\ \text{core} \in \text{Machine.cores},\ 
\forall\ \text{entry} \in \text{core.tlb},\ 
\exists\ \text{pte} \in \text{Machine.mem}\ \text{such that}\ 
\text{entry.ppn} = \text{pte.ppn} \land \text{entry.perm} \subseteq \text{pte.perm}
\]

\textsc{Tessera} proves:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Root-entry removal} (Stage 1): dropping a level-2 PTE
        faults the walk at level 2, before any address arithmetic is forced.
  \item \textbf{Leaf-entry removal} (Stage 1.1): the same guarantee for
        level-0 PTEs—invalidation at any level of the radix tree prevents
        translation.
  \item \textbf{Upstream conformance} (G1): the hand-transcribed oracle
        walk is proved equivalent to the upstream sail-riscv \texttt{pt\_walk}
        via a bitfield extraction bridge lemma.
  \item \textbf{Multi-architecture conformance}: the same coherence theorems
        are replayed for MIPS PageGrain (1~KiB pages), LoongArch (odd/even
        page-pair MMU), and AArch64 (block+contpte+LPA2) via generic
        shootdown modules.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{TLB Shootdown Under Weak Memory (G3--G4)}
\label{sec:shootdown_proofs}

The shootdown protocol—invalidate PTE, local flush, IPI broadcast, wait
for remote acknowledgements—is proven correct under genuine weak-memory
semantics:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Sequential consistency (S2.0).}] The functional shootdown
        theorem: if every core flushes its TLB after PTE invalidation, no
        core can translate the VA. Proved for Sv39, MIPS, LoongArch, and
        AArch64.

  \item[\textbf{N-core weak memory (S2.2).}] The broadcast protocol
        is lifted to the gpfsl/iRC11 release-acquire model
        \footcite{kaiser18irc11} over the concrete generated
        \texttt{machine.v}. The release/acquire fences in the IPI
        mechanism are proved sufficient for coherence without requiring
        sequential consistency.

  \item[\textbf{IPI delivery model (S2.3--S2.5).}] The interrupt
        controller (INTC) is modelled as a ghost step in the gpfsl
        program: the leader's ghost step on remote ack~$i$ corresponds
        exactly to \texttt{receive\_ipi (deliver\_ipi \_ i)}. The
        interrupt priority/masking discipline and drain behaviour are
        proved not to break the broadcast invariant.
\end{description}

\subsection{IOMMU Integrity (G5)}

The IOMMU's translation cache (IOTLB) must stay coherent with the CPU
page table. \textsc{Tessera} proves the invariant $\text{IOTLB}
\subseteq \text{mapping}$ across three IOMMU architectures:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{VT-d} (Intel): root-table, context-entry, PASID-table,
        and IOTLB coherence, including ATS/PRI device-TLB shootdown under
        weak memory.
  \item \textbf{SMMUv3} (Arm): Stream Table Entry (STE), Context
        Descriptor (CD), two-stage translation, and TLBI granularity.
  \item \textbf{AMD-Vi}: Device Table Entry (DTE), PASID cache, and
        IOTLB invalidation replay.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Device Models \& Topology}

Beyond the MMU and IOMMU, \textsc{Tessera} models the interrupt controller
(GIC/AIA), timer device (SSTC), UART, network interface, and disc DMA
controller as Sail specifications with Rocq test vectors. The topology
model (SSG-1/9) captures SMT harts, NUMA nodes, and multikernel domain
boundaries for page-sharing and shootdown-scoping proofs.

\section{Machine Interface Iris Layer}
\label{sec:machine_interface}

The critical bridge between the hardware proofs and any kernel
implementation is the \textbf{machine interface Iris layer}—a set of
Iris separation-logic resources that encode hardware invariants as
proof-carrying tokens:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
(* "I own this PTE and it maps va → pa with permissions perm" *)
Definition pte_token (va : vaddr) (pa : paddr) (perm : Perm)
  : iProp Σ := ...

(* "I flushed this TLB entry; no core can translate va" *)
Definition tlb_flushed (va : vaddr) : iProp Σ := ...

(* "The IOMMU domain d translates va → pa" *)
Definition iommu_mapping (d : Domain) (va : vaddr) (pa : paddr)
  : iProp Σ := ...

(* "This PTE subtree is shared across N address spaces" *)
Definition shared_subtree (root_ppn : paddr) (n : nat)
  : iProp Σ := ...
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

Each resource is \textbf{proved sound} against the generated machine
model. For example:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
Lemma wp_sfence_vma (va : vaddr) :
  {{{ pte_token va pa perm ∗ ... }}}
    sfence_vma va
  {{{ RET (); tlb_flushed va }}}.

Lemma unmap_protocol_preserves_coherence (va : vaddr) :
  pte_token va pa perm ∗ tlb_entry va ⊢
  |==> tlb_flushed va ∗ ¬pte_token va pa perm.
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

The \texttt{unmap\_protocol\_preserves\_coherence} lemma is \textbf{not}
a kernel-specific proof. It composes the hardware theorems
(\texttt{bc\_broadcast\_spec}, \texttt{coherence\_leaf},
\texttt{iommu\_shootdown\_correct}) into a protocol-level guarantee:
\textit{any} code that acquires \texttt{pte\_token} and follows the
four-step protocol (invalidate → local flush → IPI → wait) is entitled to
\texttt{tlb\_flushed}. The kernel's job is to follow the protocol.

\section{Kernel Verification via RefinedRust}
\label{sec:kernel_verification}

\subsection{Toolchain Selection}

Two distinct approaches exist for connecting Rust kernel code to Rocq
proofs.  The automatic approach (RefinedRust
\footcite{gaher24refinedrust}, Aeneas \footcite{ho22aeneas}) translates
Rust MIR directly to Coq.  This is appealing in principle but has not
been demonstrated on bare-metal kernel code: no \texttt{std}, raw MMIO,
inline assembly (\texttt{sfence.vma}, \texttt{mret}), a custom allocator,
and interrupt-driven state machines all lie outside the demonstrated
comfort zone of these tools.

Telix therefore adopts the \textbf{manual Iris heap\_lang} approach:
the kernel's behaviour is specified by hand in Iris's built-in language
(heap\_lang), in the style of the seL4 verification in Isabelle/HOL
\footcite{klei09}.  The advantages:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{No toolchain risk.}  The Iris language is a known,
    stable target with a published semantics.  There is no dependence
    on a Rust MIR→Coq translator whose bare-metal behaviour is
    uncharacterised.
  \item \textbf{Direct composition with Tessera.}  The same separation
    logic, same proof assistant, same weak-memory model (gpfsl/iRC11)
    used in the hardware proofs is used for the kernel proofs.
  \item \textbf{Auditable gap.}  The semantic gap between the Iris
    spec and the Rust implementation is a single text (the Iris
    program) that is read and audited, not a compiler pipeline whose
    translation correctness must itself be proved.
  \item \textbf{Assembly specialisation from Sail.}  Privileged
    instructions (\texttt{sfence.vma}, \texttt{mret}, \texttt{csrw
    satp}, interrupt entry/exit) are \emph{specialised} from the
    existing Sail machine model's executable semantics, rather than
    independently axiomatised.  This keeps the kernel's assembly
    specification consistent with the hardware proofs by construction.
\end{itemize}

The cost: each kernel function must be modelled by hand, which is slower
per function than an automatic translation.  The scope is bounded:
the framekernel core is \textasciitilde{}3,000--5,000 lines of Rust,
and not all of it is protocol-relevant.

