\textbf{Manual Iris is the chosen path} because the scope is already
vast and the toolchain risk of automatic translation is not worth
bearing.  This is the same decision seL4 made (manual Isabelle
specification rather than an automatic C-to-Isabelle translator), and
for the same reason: the kernel is small enough that manual
specification is tractable, and the semantic gap must be auditable.

\begin{itemize}
  \item The machine interface layer (\S\ref{sec:machine_interface}) can be
        directly imported as Iris resources into RefinedRust's Coq model
        of the kernel.
  \item A kernel function like \texttt{unmap\_range()} gets an Iris spec:
        $\{\ \texttt{pte\_token}(va, pa, perm) *
           \texttt{tlb\_entry}(va)\ \}\
           \texttt{unmap\_range}(va)\
           \{\ \texttt{tlb\_flushed}(va) *
           \neg\texttt{pte\_token}(va, pa, perm)\ \}$.
  \item The hardware's \texttt{bc\_broadcast\_spec} theorem guarantees
        that following the protocol achieves
        $\texttt{tlb\_flushed}(va)$. RefinedRust proves the kernel follows
        the protocol. The two proofs compose.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Scope and Limitations}

The target is the \textbf{full framekernel core}: page-table traversal
and modification, TLB shootdown protocol, IPI send/receive, IOTLB
invalidation, the scheduler management loop, and the LLFree allocator
(\textasciitilde{}3,000--5,000 lines of Rust).  The rationale is that
the verification exists to address the vulnerability class identified
by Mythos et al.; a partial core is insufficient.

The proof proceeds layer by layer.  The machine interface Iris layer
(\S\ref{sec:machine_interface}) is the first deliverable, establishing
that the hardware theorems compose into coherent Iris resources.  Kernel
functions are then proved against those resources function by function.

The external pagers, personality servers, filesystem, and scheduler's
user-facing EEVDF loop are verified separately or treated as untrusted.

\subsection{How the Whitepaper Design Reduces Verification Surface}

Each design choice in Chapters~\ref{ch:memory} and~\ref{ch:control-plane} simplifies the
verification task:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{Design Choice} & \textbf{Verification Benefit} \\
\midrule
External pagers (VM in userspace)
  & Each pager is a separate process; its proof is independent and
    bounded. A fault in one pager cannot corrupt the framekernel. \\
\midrule
Coremapless (no global \texttt{mem\_map[]})
  & No global invariant over all frames; per-frame ownership via
    LLFree tokens. Page ownership is local, not global. \\
\midrule
Continuation-passing style (CPS)
  & Rust \texttt{Future} continuations have explicit, finite state
    sets—easier to specify than arbitrary stackful code. Known Iris
    models exist from the RustBelt project. \\
\midrule
Personality servers (Linux/Windows/Zircon)
  & Foreign ABIs are untrusted. Correctness depends only on capability
    channel protocols, not on the internal logic of any server. \\
\midrule
Framekernel shared address space
  & No address-space switch during IPC. TLB coherence is the
    \emph{only} cross-domain concern requiring proof. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{How whitepaper design choices reduce the verification surface area.}
\label{tab:design_verification}
\end{table}

\subsection{Incremental Verification Strategy}

Unlike a monolithic kernel that must be verified in one
all-or-nothing pass, Telix's architecture supports incremental
verification:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Phase 1---Protocol contracts.} Extract the FSM
        description of each kernel operation's interaction with the
        machine: PTE invalidate, local flush, IPI broadcast, wait for
        acks. Prove that executing the protocol restores coherence
        (composing hardware theorems). These proofs are reusable by any
        kernel implementation.

  \item \textbf{Phase 2---PMC instrumentation.} Insert lightweight
        trace assertions into the existing prototype that log protocol
        state transitions at runtime. A single test run exercises all
        kernel operations and provides a witness that the protocol was
        followed.

  \item \textbf{Phase 3---Machine interface Iris layer.} Define the
        Iris resources (\S\ref{sec:machine_interface}) and prove they are
        sound against the generated machine model.

  \item \textbf{Phase 4---Kernel proofs (manual Iris).} Specify
        the framekernel core's protocol-relevant paths in Iris
        heap\_lang and prove they satisfy the machine interface specs
        from Phase~3.  Proceed function by function, starting with
        \texttt{unmap\_range} and \texttt{shootdown\_broadcast}.
        Target scope: the full framekernel core.

  \item \textbf{Phase 5---External pager proofs.} Each pager (COW,
        ZFOD, page cache, KAU manager) gets its own, independent
        proof. The pager's spec is ``if the kernel gives me
        \texttt{pte\_token}, I will manage COW/faults correctly.''
\end{enumerate}

\section{Cross-Prover Refinement}

The \textsc{Tessera} hardware proofs are in Rocq; Telix's algorithmic
proofs about KAU integrity, refcount discipline, and allocation
correctness (the ``Layer-A'' proofs) are in Lean. These are not required
to run in the same prover. The bridge is a joint invariant:

\begin{quote}
\emph{A KAU (allocation unit of size $Z$) can only be freed after the
shootdown protocol has completed for every mapping fragment $K$
within it.}
\end{quote}

This is a paper proof that does not require running both provers
simultaneously. It connects the Lean-side guarantee (``if you follow
allocation discipline, refcounts are sound'') with the Rocq-side
guarantee (``if you follow the shootdown protocol, TLBs are coherent'').

\section{Weak-Memory Model Soundness}

The weakest link in any kernel verification is the memory model.
\textsc{Tessera}'s shootdown and IOMMU proofs use the
\textbf{gpfsl/iRC11} release-acquire model \footcite{kaiser18irc11}
rather than assuming sequential consistency. This is critical because
Telix targets AArch64 and RISC-V, both of which have weakly-ordered
memory, and the framekernel relies on release/acquire fences (not
heavyweight \texttt{dsb}/\texttt{fence} barriers) for IPI
synchronisation.

The iRC11 model is an operational semantics with a race detector for
non-atomics, proven sound against the RC11 axiomatic model in prior
work. Telix does not re-prove this soundness; it inherits it from the
gpfsl library.

\section{Toolchain Availability}

All software in the verification stack is open-source and actively
maintained as of 2025--2026:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Rocq} (formerly Coq) 9.2+ with the rocq-9.2 opam switch.
  \item \textbf{Iris} 4.5.0 (heap\_lang + proofmode), with gpfsl
        vendored into \textsc{Tessera}'s \texttt{third\_party/}.
  \item \textbf{Sail} 0.20+ (sail\_coq\_backend) for ISA model generation.
  \item \textbf{RefinedRust} (PLDI 2024), available via opam.
  \item \textbf{gpfsl} (iRC11), development version compatible with
        Rocq 9.2.0.

  \item \textbf{Lean 4} (for Layer-A KAU/refcount proofs; separate opam
        switch).
\end{itemize}

\textsc{Tessera}'s build is a single script
(\texttt{hardware/rocq/build.sh}) that compiles every module
(\textasciitilde{}12,000 lines of Rocq) in \textasciitilde{}8 minutes
on a modern laptop, with automatic axiom-hygiene checking. The full
hardware-trust line—upstream Sail models $\to$ generated Rocq $\to$ pure
proofs $\to$ gpfsl weak-memory proofs $\to$ multi-arch replay—is in one
opam switch, one proof assistant, one build command.